<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Xueyantuo</h1><p class="st-meta">628 CE – 646 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Tiele tribe that briefly ruled the Mongolian steppe after the fall of the Eastern Turks, before Tang China destroyed it in 646.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Xueyantuo were one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiele">Tiele</a> tribes of the steppe, subject to the Turkic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagans</a>. In 628 they rebelled against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eastern-turkic-khaganate">Eastern Turkic Khaganate</a> under their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhenzhu-khagan">Yinan</a>, who was recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Tang Taizong</a> as Zhenzhu Khagan. After the Turks fell in 630 they became the dominant power in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mongolia">Mongolia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhenzhu-khagan">Zhenzhu Khagan</a> ruled a confederation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiele">Tiele</a> tribes including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>, and could reportedly field 200,000 horsemen. He balanced between alliance and rivalry with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a>, negotiating a marriage alliance that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Taizong</a> ultimately cancelled, and clashed with Turks resettled by the Tang south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobi-desert">Gobi</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhenzhu-khagan">Zhenzhu Khagan's</a> death in 645, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duomi-khagan">Duomi Khagan</a> attacked <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> territory. In 646 a Tang army, aided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiele">Tiele</a> tribes, destroyed the Xueyantuo. Their lands came under Tang <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a>, and the Uyghurs later rose to dominate the same region.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="khanate, 628 CE – 646 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M18 74 V58 Q50 30 82 58 V74 Z"/><path d="M42 74 V60 H58 V74"/><path d="M30 50 L50 36 L70 50"/><path d="M50 36 V26"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">khanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central Asia and the Steppe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:72.20%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>628 CE – 646 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zhenzhu Khagan, Duomi Khagan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Syr-Tardush, Seyanto</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=637&amp;focus=xueyantuo" aria-label="Xueyantuo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7135 545 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Xueyantuo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7771 1192h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7455 801h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7455 801h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Mongolia. <a href="/atlas?year=637&amp;focus=xueyantuo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

